<TopicPage slug="subjects/artificial-intelligence/intermediate/image-recognition-cnns">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are <strong>neural networks built for images</strong>:
    small learnable filters slide across the picture, stacking into layers that detect edges,
    then textures, then objects. The design borrows the visual cortex's own trick — local
    receptive fields, shared weights, hierarchies — and it is why computers can find cats,
    tumours and pedestrians. To a computer an image is just a grid of numbers — for a
    224×224 colour photo, 224 × 224 × 3 ≈ 150,000 values — and the whole problem of vision
    is turning that raw pixel soup into concepts like "pedestrian". The CNN is the
    architecture that made that translation learnable at scale.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the CNN pipeline, from filter to hierarchy to revolution" widget="equationStep" config={cnnCfg} />

  <h2>Images as numbers, and the convolution operation</h2>
  <p>
    A grayscale image is a 2-D array of brightness values; a colour image is three of them
    (red, green, blue channels). A <strong>convolution</strong> takes a tiny array of weights
    — a kernel, typically 3×3 — and slides it over the image. At each position it multiplies
    the kernel against the 3×3 patch of pixels beneath it, sums the products, and writes one
    number: the output <strong>feature map</strong>. Wherever the image locally resembles the
    pattern the kernel encodes, the output is large.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worked example. Take the patch [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]] and the classic vertical
    edge detector [[−1, 0, 1], [−1, 0, 1], [−1, 0, 1]]. The dot product is (−1+3) + (−4+6) +
    (−7+9) = 2 + 2 + 2 = 6. A bright right side against a dark left side produces a large
    positive response — the filter has found a vertical brightness change. In early CNNs some
    learned filters look almost exactly like this by hand-crafted example; the point of deep
    learning is that the network <em>derives</em> the useful filters from data instead of an
    engineer guessing them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why slide one small kernel instead of connecting every pixel to every neuron? Two
    reasons, and the second is the reason CNNs exist. First, images are local: pixel
    neighbourhoods are where structure lives. Second, <strong>weight sharing</strong>: a cat
    can appear anywhere in the frame, so one edge detector should run at every position.
    Compare parameter counts. A fully-connected hidden layer of 1,000 units over that
    224×224×3 image needs about 150,000 × 1,000 ≈ 150 million weights. A convolutional
    layer of 64 different 3×3 filters over 3 channels needs 64 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 1,728 weights.
    Four orders of magnitude fewer — cheaper to train, harder to overfit, and structurally
    committed to the right prior: the same pattern matters wherever it appears. (Two
    engineering details complete the picture: <em>padding</em> adds a border so the output
    map stays the same size, and <em>stride</em> skips positions to shrink it.)
  </p>

  <h2>Pooling and the hierarchy: how depth buys abstraction</h2>
  <p>
    After each convolution, a <strong>pooling</strong> layer summarises small neighbourhoods:
    max-pooling keeps only the strongest activation in each 2×2 patch. The map shrinks by a
    factor of 4, small translations stop mattering (a cat that moved two pixels still
    triggers the same pooled unit), and the next layer works on a coarser view. Stack
    convolve-pool-convolve-pool and something remarkable emerges. Layer 1's feature maps
    respond to edges, blobs and colour gradients — at every position. Layer 2 composes those
    into corners, stripes and textures. Layer 3 responds to object parts — eyes, wheels,
    windows. Deeper layers respond to whole objects. Each layer's 3×3 view of the previous
    layer's features amounts to a much larger <em>effective</em> receptive field on the
    original image — a neuron at layer 5 can "see" a quarter of the picture.
  </p>
  <p>
    Crucially, nobody specifies this hierarchy. The architecture only says "local filters,
    shared, stacked"; the specific features emerge from training on labelled images. When
    researchers visualised what trained networks had learned (deconvolution
    reconstructions, Zeiler &amp; Fergus 2014; occlusion experiments), the hierarchy was
    there, self-organised — the vindication of the approach. The biological echo is real:
    Hubel and Wiesel's Nobel-winning work (1959–62) found simple cells in cat visual cortex
    responding to oriented edges and complex cells tolerant to position, and Fukushima's
    neocognitron (1980) translated that circuit idea into a layered network; LeCun's LeNet
    (1989–98) added backpropagation and put CNNs to work reading bank cheque digits.
  </p>

  <h2>ReLU, and the ImageNet moment</h2>
  <p>
    Depth stalled for years because of the plumbing: early neuron activations (sigmoids)
    squashed gradients as they flowed backward through many layers, so deep networks
    trained slowly or not at all. The <strong>rectified linear unit</strong> — ReLU, simply
    max(0, x) — changed that: it does not saturate for positive inputs, its gradient is
    constant 1 on the active side, and it is nearly free to compute. Around the same time,
    GPUs (built for video-game shading, which is just matrix arithmetic) offered parallel
    compute at consumer prices.
  </p>
  <p>
    These pieces converged in 2012, when Krizhevsky, Sutskever and Hinton's
    <strong>AlexNet</strong> entered the ImageNet Large Scale Visual Recognition Challenge
    — 1.2 million training images, 1,000 categories, scored by top-5 error (is the true
    label among the model's five best guesses?). AlexNet reached 15.3% against the runner-up's
    26.2% — a drop of eleven points in one year, delivered by two gaming GPUs, ReLUs,
    dropout regularisation and clever data handling. Within two years every serious vision
    lab had pivoted; by 2015 residual networks (ResNet) had pushed top-5 error to 3.6%,
    below the roughly 5% a careful human achieves on the same task. The field calls this
    the ImageNet moment because it is when "deep learning" stopped being a curiosity and
    became the default method.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the network "sees" the way you do. Visualisation
  experiments suggest otherwise — CNNs lean heavily on texture and local statistics where
  humans use shape and context. A lesioned texture can fool both human and machine in
  opposite ways, and models confidently misclassify objects photographed in unusual poses.
  The networks solve the task they were trained on with features that are statistically
  effective, not human-like.</p>

  <h2>From classification to detection, segmentation and transfer</h2>
  <p>
    Whole-image classification ("this photo contains a dog") is only the base task.
    <strong>Object detection</strong> both localises and labels many objects per image with
    bounding boxes — YOLO-family models do it in one forward pass, fast enough for video,
    which is what makes self-driving perception and photo search possible.
    <strong>Semantic segmentation</strong> goes further and labels every pixel — the
    boundary of the tumour, not just "tumour somewhere" — and is medical imaging's
    workhorse (U-Net-style encoder-decoder architectures dominate). Radiologists use these
    systems as second readers; the honest framing is a tireless assistant with superhuman
    consistency in narrow tasks and superhuman fragility outside them.
  </p>
  <p>
    And almost nobody trains vision networks from scratch any more.
    <strong>Transfer learning</strong> starts from a network pretrained on millions of
    labelled images and fine-tunes it on your few hundred — the early layers' edge and
    texture knowledge transfers to X-rays, satellite photos or factory defects. This is
    practical necessity (labelled data is expensive) and also a subtle risk vector:
    whatever quirks and biases the source dataset encoded arrive free with the weights,
    and evaluation on the new task will not necessarily reveal them.
  </p>

  <h2>Known failure modes</h2>
  <p>
    Three stand out, and each has changed practice. <strong>Adversarial examples</strong>:
    add a mathematically crafted perturbation, invisible to humans, and a strong classifier
    flips its answer with high confidence (the panda relabelled "gibbon" in the 2017
    demonstration). The model has found decision boundaries humans do not share — a
    security problem for anything adversarial actors can touch (road signs included).
    <strong>Texture bias and shortcut learning</strong>: models exploit correlations —
    a "skin lesion classifier" that learned ruler marks present in malignant-photo datasets;
    a "sheep detector" that keyed on grass. If the training data contains a shortcut, the
    optimiser will take it. <strong>Distribution shift</strong>: a model trained on one
    hospital's scanners loses accuracy on another's; benchmarks are not deployment. The
    professional response to all three is the same discipline: evaluate on data that looks
    like the real world, test the failure cases on purpose, and monitor after launch —
    because a broken model fails silently.
  </p>

  <h2>Data is the other half of the model</h2>
  <p>
    Architecture gets the glory, but the dataset decides the ceiling. ImageNet itself was a
    product of labour as much as insight: over 14 million images, organised by WordNet
    categories, largely labelled by crowdsourced Amazon Mechanical Turk workers — Fei-Fei
    Li's argument being that the field was algorithm-rich and data-poor. The 2012 result
    was as much a dataset event as an architecture event. Three data-side practices then
    matter as much as any layer design. <strong>Data augmentation</strong> — random crops,
    flips, colour jitters — manufactures the invariances you want the network to learn,
    for free. <strong>Label noise and dataset bias</strong> propagate silently: benchmarks
    overweight Western, well-lit, photogenic imagery, and models trained on them
    underperform systematically on other populations (face-analysis error rates differing
    across skin tones — the documented "gender shades" findings — are the canonical
    warning). And <strong>evaluation design</strong> is a data decision: a held-out test
    set from the same collection as training flatters the model; a test set from new
    hospitals, new cameras, new years, tells the truth. In applied vision, the honest
    ordering is usually: dataset first, architecture second.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The degradation problem and skip connections.</strong> Before 2015, deeper
    plain networks sometimes performed <em>worse</em> than shallower ones even on training
    data — not classic overfitting but an optimisation pathology (gradients fading or
    exploding through the stack). ResNet's fix was architecturally simple and profound:
    each block adds its output to its input (a skip connection), so a layer only needs to
    learn the <em>correction</em> to the identity map. Suddenly 50-, 101-, even 152-layer
    networks trained cleanly, and residual connections became standard equipment in
    transformers too.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What a conv layer actually computes, quantitatively.</strong> The compute budget
    matters for deployment: a 3×3 filter over a 224×224 map costs 9 multiply-adds per output
    position per channel — one reason mobile and embedded vision uses reduced-precision
    arithmetic, pruned networks and architectures designed for the phone, not the data
    centre. Real-time video perception is as much an engineering achievement in arithmetic
    economy as a modelling one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The successor question.</strong> Convolution's translation sharing is one prior;
    attention (as in Vision Transformers, from 2020) offers a different one — every patch
    can consult every other, with positional encodings supplying the geometry. On enough
    data, transformers match or beat CNNs at classification and now dominate vision-language
    tasks; CNNs remain the efficiency champions and their inductive bias still wins when
    data is scarce. The current synthesis: hybrid and pretraining-heavy approaches, where
    architectures matter less than the scale and diversity of what they see.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open problems.</strong> Robustness is still unsolved — no vision system matches
    human reliability under adversarial or shifted inputs. Causality is untouched: a CNN
    learns correlations in pixels, not "what would happen if". And interpretability has not
    settled: saliency maps show where a model looked, not always why it decided. Vision is
    the field where deep learning won first, and it remains the field where the gap between
    benchmark and understanding is most instructive.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> LeCun et al. (1998) LeNet; Krizhevsky, Sutskever &amp; Hinton (2012); Zeiler &amp; Fergus (2014); He et al. (2015) ResNet; Goodfellow, Bengio &amp; Courville, Deep Learning ch. 9; Russell &amp; Norvig, AIMA chs. 21, 25; Szegedy et al. (2013) on adversarial examples.</p>

